-- Chapter: Genesis 37 — ★ KEYSTONE: THE JOSEPH CYCLE BEGINS — JOSEPH AS THE SUFFERING-THEN-EXALTED MESSIANIC TYPE.
--   Jacob loved Joseph more than all his children and made him a coat of many colours (v.3); his brethren hated him
--   and could not speak peaceably to him (v.4). The dreams: the sheaves bow (v.7), the sun and moon and eleven stars
--   make obeisance (v.9) — exaltation foretold of the one rejected. The conspiracy: "Behold, this dreamer cometh...
--   let us slay him... and we shall see what will become of his dreams" (v.18-20). Reuben would save him (v.21-22);
--   Yahudah (Judah) says "What profit is it if we slay our brother... let us sell him... for he is our brother and
--   our flesh" (v.26-27); sold for TWENTY PIECES OF SILVER to the Ishmeelites/Midianites and brought into Egypt
--   (v.28,36). Stripped of the coat (v.23), cast into the pit with no water (v.24), the coat dipped in goat's blood
--   (v.31), the father's mourning "I will go down into the grave (sheol) unto my son" (v.35). Read FORWARD to
--   Yahusha (Jesus): the rejected-then-raised Messiah. Read as TYPE through the Formed-and-Formless lens — Joseph
--   foreshadows the One who would be received not, sold, presumed dead, and raised to save the very brethren who
--   rejected him; never a flattening of the Formed Son into a mere man, never a co-equal-persons grammar.
